Parser
------

Parser:
	Pros and cons
	Generic:
		+ modify grammar file, no code change
		+ can do grammar validation
		- C++ data based grammar not trivial
		- not flexible grammar
		- automatic error making (not always clear)
		- automatic parse tree has to be converted again later
	Custom:
		- more to type
		- more changes to make manually if grammar changes
		+ custom diagnostics
		+ custom grammar adjustments for non LL(1)
		+ direct "final" ast building
		+ slightly faster
	
	Parser is an external object that builds ast components from the outside and assembles them together. AST objects have only introspection stuff mostly. They lose most of the concrete syntax, retain only SourcePos'es for diagnostics.
	AST objects are as simple as possible. Avoid data replication, prefer algorithmic lookup instead (optimise later).

During parsing, types are kept as direct equivalent of the syntax:

TypeDecl
	enum num, bool, text, data, opaque, named
	"named" is for classes and structs
	Also has a list of typedecls for template parameters, if any

This object can be used to represent the name of a class and indexing (thanks to a "to string" method)

Then at analysis, they are resolved and replaced by internized objects that point directly at the right classes
	
Type
	ClassType (points to class, possibly instance of template or built-in)
	MultiType (to deal with expr-list logics)
	VoidType

And a Class can be in fact the instanciation of a class template. A template is just a class with the information "generic types" filled with the names. When there is none, it's an actual class.

Templates are stored in another place. Instantiated templates are however stored with the real classes. Whenever we encounter a templated type in code, we build the instantiation and add it to the list of classes (and run validation on it).
At analysis, the instanciated classes wrap the functionality of the template they point to, replacing the parameters by actual types on the fly. Somehow. In templates, the parameter types stay as TypeDecl. Only in instances, they become actual types.

Class
	TemplateInstance (points at template and at the specialised types. really only shares interface with Class, implementation is very different)

Expression is anything that has a type

Expression
	Cast
	Message
		Explicit (including single 'Name', that can be replaced by LocalAccess)
		Indexing
		BinOp
		UnOp
		(this might be an enum in a single MethodCall object)
	Constant
	Constructor
	MultiExpression (keep it a single object to avoid duplicating the list logic. Used for assignment RHS, call arguments, return values)
	ExpressionSelect (points at a multityped expression and has an index to chose which element. Used to wrap right side in assignments. Selecting in a MultiExpression should always return the smallest inner expression possible: "unfolding")

Local

Statement
	(control structures)
	Expression
	MultiAssignment (the one we parse, then specialises into LocalAssignments and MethodCall's (for setter methods))
	LocalAssignment (points at separate Local)

A multiassignment is split into individual assignments (variable or setter call). The right-hand side is split using individual expressions, or ExpressionSelect to split return values of method calls. Each sub assignment is self sufficient.
Method
Member (just a small struct in Class)
Block

Blocks have lists of locals and can look them up. Scopes point at outer scope for recursive lookup

Arguments derive from Local Variables, and are stuck directly into the methods' bodies. Only their types are gathered at method level for interface building. The "self" local is added automatically.

Classes point at their parent, but inheritence is done only by recursive lookup (no gathering of members and methods). At analysis, overriding methods and shared members get flags set.

For tree modification, all analysis functions are outside the object, so it's enough to pass a reference to the pointer to the node, to be able to delete the old one and replace it by a new built.

Context is the enclosing object for the internal representation of a program. It contains a list of classes, a list of templates
